 "url": "https://factory.ai/news/missions-architecture",
 "verdicts": {
  "A Slack-clone mission ran 16.5 hours: orchestration 2.3%, implementation 60.5%, validation 37.2% of runtime, with 185 total agent runs (63 workers, 27 validators).": 1,
  "A programmatic runner spawns one fresh-context worker per feature in order; when all features in a milestone are complete, the runner triggers validation with fresh agents.": 1,
  "After validation, the orchestrator creates targeted fix features that run before the milestone re-validates, looping until validation passes; if implementation or validation is blocked, the orchestrator halts the mission and returns control to the user.": 1,
  "At the mission level the orchestrator writes a validation contract (behavioral assertions defining success) before defining any features, so the contract is not influenced by the planned implementation.": 1,
  "Factory's Missions system breaks large work into focused units handled by fresh agents with narrowly scoped goals, shared state, and explicit validation, because a single agent's context window cannot hold most real projects.": 1,
  "Factory's design rationale is that agents are highly reactive to their context: a trajectory is append-only, models seek coherence, and irrelevant or adversarial accumulated context degrades performance.": 1,
  "In Missions the orchestrator plans and decomposes the user's goal, delegates all investigation and implementation to subagents and workers, and does not drive validation directly; the system injects validators at milestones.": 1,
  "In the Slack-clone mission every milestone converged in 2-4 validation rounds; validators surfaced 81 issues (65 blocking) and the orchestrator created 21 fix features, 34.4% of 61 implementation features.": 1,
  "In the Slack-clone mission median trajectory length was 51 turns for implementation (p90 123) and 30 turns for validation (p90 37).": 1,
  "Missions allows model choice per role: broad planning and judgment for the orchestrator, reliable and cost-efficient execution for workers, and thoroughness and skepticism for validators.": 1,
  "Missions state is externalized into shared artifacts: validation-contract.md, features.json, services.yaml, AGENTS.md, research notes, operational guidelines, and an evolving knowledge base.": 1,
  "Missions uses two validator types: scrutiny validators review each worker's implementation and trajectory and encode knowledge updates into shared state, and user-testing validators exercise the system as a black box against the validation contract.": 1,
  "Missions validators do not implement fixes; they surface issues to the orchestrator, which creates fix features that future workers implement.": 1,
  "Missions workers complete well-specified features with clear success criteria and write tests before code, but an independent validator, not the worker, makes the final judgment on correctness.": 1,
  "The Slack-clone mission used 778.5M tokens (744.9M cache read) and produced 38.8k lines of code, 52.5% of them tests, with 89.25% statement coverage.": 1
 }
}